        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe field/getter/setter on `Owner` (mirroring `possibleDuplicate` et al.), the `DeleteOwner` soft-delete, the four schema files, and the OpenAPI addition are all clean, additive, and idiomatic. The problem is where the \"ignore soft-deleted owners\" rule lives.\n\n**The exclusion is an inline one-off, but it's a cross-cutting query concern applied to only one of many identical scans.**\n\nThis codebase has ~9 functions that scan `ownerRepository.findAll()` and conceptually reason over \"existing/current owners\": `RequireUniqueIdentity`, `AssignOwnerPossibleDuplicate`, `AssignOwnerNamesakeCount`, `RequireCityCapacity`, `RequireDailyOwnerLimit`, `AssignOwnerBulkSignupWarning`, `OwnerHousehold`, `DeduplicateOwnerCustomerCode`, `ListOwners`. The change teaches exactly one of them (`RequireUniqueIdentity`) to skip deleted rows via an inline `if (existing.isDeleted()) continue;`. That creates two structural problems a maintainer would trip over:\n\n1. **Inconsistency with a sibling in the same pipeline.** `AssignOwnerPossibleDuplicate` (self-described as part of the create-owner duplicate logic \u2014 it scans existing owners for a household match and stamps `possibleDuplicateOf`) still counts soft-deleted owners as match candidates. Two adjacent create-pipeline functions now scan the same owner set with opposite deleted-visibility semantics. The spec frames \"the create endpoint's duplicate and identity checks\" as one rule; splitting it so only the identity scan honors it is incoherent.\n\n2. **Duplication-prone convention.** Encoding \"skip deleted\" as a per-call-site loop guard means every current and future owner scan must independently remember to re-add it. That's the sort of ad-hoc filter that drifts out of sync.\n\n**Suggested direction:** put the soft-delete visibility rule in a shared unit rather than inline \u2014 e.g. a repository query/method that returns non-deleted owners (or a shared `Owner`/predicate helper), so identity, possible-duplicate, and any other \"existing owners\" scan get consistent semantics. `findById` stays as-is so `GET /api/owners/{id}` still returns the deleted owner, satisfying the spec. At minimum, apply the same exclusion to `AssignOwnerPossibleDuplicate` so the create endpoint's duplicate handling is internally consistent."
